<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/43914c4e-398c-44ed-b04b-0828c47ce54a> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Out-of-scope CodeEntities: mint reports, an explicit tool prunes" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/43914c4e-398c-44ed-b04b-0828c47ce54a> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#concerns> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/CodeEntity/14b273c2-6595-42fe-8e1a-1b0a5a735f90> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/43914c4e-398c-44ed-b04b-0828c47ce54a> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#concerns> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/SystemComponent/11e01ea2-f520-4f7c-a165-09532dacd43f> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/43914c4e-398c-44ed-b04b-0828c47ce54a> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#concerns> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/SystemComponent/accf4f9f-6309-4d3f-8705-26e551824eb0> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/43914c4e-398c-44ed-b04b-0828c47ce54a> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/43914c4e-398c-44ed-b04b-0828c47ce54a> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "Claim: `MintPlan::out_of_scope` reports every minted CodeEntity whose symbol is still a live workspace definition but which the batch-mint scope rule no longer covers, and deletion happens only in `examples/prune_out_of_scope_code_entities.rs`, gated on three conjuncts. Batch minting itself never deletes.\n\nTHE HOLE THIS CLOSES. Orphaning is judged against ALL workspace definitions, not the mint scope (`plan_mint`), so narrowing the scope does not orphan what a looser scope minted: those entities are not `orphaned` (their symbol exists), not `create`/`update` (never planned), and not `unchanged` (never walked). They fell out of every bucket in the report — `plan.unchanged` simply dropped by 736 with nothing accounting for it. The bucket is computed from the same `kept` set minting itself walks, so it cannot disagree with the scope actually applied.\n\nWHY DELETION IS NOT A MINT FLAG. Out-of-scope is necessary but nowhere near sufficient to delete. A lazily minted private entity is permanently out of batch scope and may hold the only link to recorded knowledge. In this store the bucket held 754 entities, of which 18 were exactly that — `definition_is_public`, `canonical_symbol`, `trusted_baseline`, `source_request_is_trusted`, `resolve_anchor_lines` and others, several carrying the only `isConcernedBy`/`isConstrainedBy` edge to an accepted ADR or Constraint. Making `mint --apply` prune would turn a routine, hook-friendly command into a destructive one, and would decide the deliberately deferred deletion policy (Consequence b9145512) as a side effect.\n\nTHE THREE CONJUNCTS. (1) in `plan.out_of_scope`; (2) the entity's asserted predicates are a SUBSET of the shape minting itself writes (rdf:type, rdfs:label, hasSubstrateSymbol, hasEntityKind, hasCodeName, hasLogicalPath, definedInPath, realizes) — a subset test, not a count, because 16 entities legitimately lack `realizes`; (3) nothing references it in the PROJECT graph, by IRI or by literal.\n\nCONJUNCT 2 IS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE, and it is not redundant with 3. `link_code` orients edges through the relation catalogue and frequently makes the CodeEntity the SUBJECT (`isConcernedBy`, `playsRole`, `hasCriticality`), so a linked entity can have ZERO inbound references. The materialized inverse that would reveal it is reasoner-derived and absent until `ensure_enriched` runs, and it is stripped from kg.nq entirely. An inbound-only rule would therefore have deleted all 18 — including the entity that the very ADR governing this predicate points at.\n\nREFERENCE CHECKS ARE GRAPH-SCOPED, INCLUDING LITERAL ONES. The project graph is committed, exported, and authoritative, so a reference there blocks — a ProposedLink names its subject as a plain string, invisible to SHACL, which is why literals are scanned at all. Derived local graphs are reported but never block. This was found empirically, not reasoned: blocking on literals in any graph produced PRUNE=0 on the first dry run, because MOOSE session traces embed entity IRIs in the recorded TEXT of past answers, and treating query history as an assertion would make every entity any query ever mentioned permanently unprunable.\n\nRESULT (2026-08-19). 736 entities removed, every one a scip-typescript Meta descriptor, zero exceptions; 10,304 quads (5,888 project, 4,416 provenance); validation conforms with 0 violations; full suite 502 passed. CodeEntity 2,459 → 1,723." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/43914c4e-398c-44ed-b04b-0828c47ce54a> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
